  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH EARLY NEXT WEEK/... \nIssued at 245 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThursday-Friday:\nAdditional weak shortwave energy translate through southwest\nflow into Thursday and Friday, though moisture return and\nforcing look to remain minimal with dry air persisting in the\nmid-levels. Ensemble guidance suggests the greatest forcing will\nbe focused north and west of the area through Friday afternoon.\nLow PoPs (10-20%) glance the area, though most of the area\nremains dry.\n\nThe warmest day of the week comes on Thursday, with current\nforecast highs in the lower 80s. Above normal temperatures\npersist through Friday and the upcoming weekend, though a\npattern change is on the horizon.\n\nSaturday-Early Next Week:\nThe mid-level trough and stronger shortwave energy move into the\ncentral CONUS this weekend. Stronger moisture influx into the\narea, as apparent with forecast PWATs around 1.25 to 1.5 inches.\nThis environment will be supportive of scattered showers and a\nfew thunderstorms (40-60% chances) overspreading the area Friday\nnight into Saturday morning from west to east. Rain chances\npersist through Saturday afternoon, with thunder/lightning\nlimited. The better instability/shear remain suppressed south\nof the area on Saturday. The recent timing suggests rain chances\ndissipate Saturday evening into the overnight, though some\nadjustments remain likely as the event nears. For those\npartaking in outdoor activities, a rain jacket may be needed\nwith scattered rain chances lingering around. Rainfall amounts\nlook to remain light with this event. NBM probabilities depict\nthe following for Friday night through Sunday:\n\nProb of > 0.10: 60-70%\nProb of > 0.25 inch: 40-50%\nProb of >0.50 inch: 30-40%\n\nDrier weather into Sunday ahead of an active pattern returning\nearly next week. Ensemble guidance vary on exact timing and\nplacement of synoptic features, but there the general message\nremains on track for increasing rain chances (40-70%) early next\nweek. Any rainfall would be beneficial to ongoing drought\nconditions, though it is too early to pin down rainfall amounts.\nHighs remain near or above normal into early next week for the\nfirst week of November.",
